WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
INTRO:
Shark Tank is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:07.560
most popular business/venture capital show in the&nbsp;
world. I mean what’s more motivating that watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.360
aspiring entrepreneurs try to pitch their ideas&nbsp;
and take their companies to the next level. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:23.700
while Shark Tank is no doubt entertaining, that’s&nbsp;
all it should be used for: entertainment. Shark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.160
Tank is a terrible representation when it comes&nbsp;
to what goes down in a real VC pitch. So much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.980
of what we see is completely staged or just for&nbsp;
the camera. Not to mention, the producers like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.720
to warp what really happens to make it more&nbsp;
entertaining for us viewers. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.820
an entrepreneur may only stumble once&nbsp;
in an hour long pitch. But given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.620
these pitches are condensed into just 10 to 15&nbsp;
minutes, it seems like their entire pitch was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.500
trainwreck. Not to mention, the business advice&nbsp;
that the Sharks give is often hit or miss. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.060
don’t get me wrong here, Shark tank is a&nbsp;
phenomenal show and I’ve been a fan for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.680
several years. But, we need to be able&nbsp;
to separate between entertainment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.480
real entrepreneurship because Shark Tank is&nbsp;
not that great when it comes to the latter.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:14.820
PUBLICITY STUNT:&nbsp;

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:20.520
Starting off with the biggest issue, no one on&nbsp;
Shark Tank is actually there to make a deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:27.720
You see, with real VCs, the only reason anyone is&nbsp;
there is to make a deal. Investors want to invest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.460
their money to make returns while entrepreneurs&nbsp;
need an investment to grow their business. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:38.820
on Shark Tank, the only reason anyone is there is&nbsp;
for publicity. If an entrepreneur is there only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:44.160
make a deal, then they’re just naive. Most of&nbsp;
them know that the second their episode airs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.440
their business is gonna blow up. This is one of&nbsp;
the reason that Shark Tank businesses have such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.200
a high success rate. In fact, only 6% of Shark&nbsp;
Tank businesses have gone out of business. Once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.860
they get some momentum from Shark Tank, they’re&nbsp;
able to achieve the economies of scale that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:04.140
they need to turn their startup into a legitimate&nbsp;
company. But, what this also means is that getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.600
an investment from the Sharks themselves is not&nbsp;
main reason they’re there. The real benefit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.760
pitching on the tank is not convincing the&nbsp;
sharks but convincing you the viewer. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.620
really it’s just one big infomercial.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, from the sharks’ perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.020
they have no monetary reason to make deals either.&nbsp;
The primary reason the sharks are there is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.300
promote their own personal brand image. I mean&nbsp;
just think about this, how much money could the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.580
sharks even make from these deals in the first&nbsp;
place. Take Mark Cuban for example. His median&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:41.760
investment size is $125,000 and his median&nbsp;
stake is 20%. Virtually none of deals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.620
he makes have any chance of reaching the billion&nbsp;
dollar mark. More times than not, these are small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.000
niche businesses that would be lucky to reach&nbsp;
the $10 million mark. Maybe the strongest out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.260
of them could reach a $100 million. Now, from&nbsp;
the entrepreneurs perspective, this is awesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:01.980
If you own a $100 million business or even a&nbsp;
$10 million business, you’re set for life. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:08.580
how much does this matter for Mark. A 20% stake&nbsp;
in a $10 million business is only $2 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:17.640
With a net worth of $5.83 billion, this increases&nbsp;
his net worth by a whopping 0.03%. Even if we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:23.220
talking about a $100 million cash out, that’s&nbsp;
only $20 million for Mark or a 0.3% increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:28.320
net worth. And that’s if his stake didn’t get&nbsp;
watered down by new investors. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.840
the other Sharks aren’t as rich as Mark Cuban,&nbsp;
but even for them, they’d be lucky to grow their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:39.060
net worths by 10% through all the investments&nbsp;
they ever make on Shark Tank. The same, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:44.160
cannot be said about the publicity they receive.&nbsp;
How many more people will recognize the Mavericks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.560
because they see Mark Cuban on TV every week? How&nbsp;
many more people will watch QVC because they saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.900
Lori on Shark Tank? How many people didn’t even&nbsp;
know about FUBU until they say Daymond on Shark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.480
Tank? This is where the real money is for the&nbsp;
Sharks. Take the Mavericks for example. Until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.340
Shark Tank started gaining traction, they were&nbsp;
worth about $400 million. But, over the last 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:12.300
years, they’ve grown all the way to $2.7 billion&nbsp;
which is a $2.3 billion profit for Cuban. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.700
you could argue that this gain in valuation is&nbsp;
due to the team itself becoming better. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.260
to that I would argue that the main reason the&nbsp;
team became better was because of the publicity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.120
Mark got the team. As the Mavericks become more&nbsp;
recognizable, they become a more desirable team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.200
to play for which of course attracts better&nbsp;
talent. This same argument could be made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.420
for the brand values of FUBU and the Herjavec&nbsp;
group as well. Clearly, for both the sharks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:42.360
the entrepreneurs, the money is not in the deal,&nbsp;
it’s in the publicity. So, how could such a show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.700
possibly set realistic expectations regarding&nbsp;
venture capital for aspiring entrepreneurs.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:51.900
SUCCESS PORN:
Given that much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.340
the show is just one big publicity stunt, I&nbsp;
don’t think you’d be surprised to hear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.500
a significant portion of the deals that are made&nbsp;
on air never make it to the other side. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.560
with most sharks the odds of the deal being&nbsp;
completed are basically just a coinflip. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:14.580
shark that has a high completion rate is Mark who&nbsp;
comes in at 87.5%. And honestly, this is likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:19.860
a strategic play by Mark as well. The investments&nbsp;
that he makes are not consequential to his wealth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.320
so being a man of his word is more important than&nbsp;
potentially making a bad investment. The average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:30.780
viewer will likely never see these stats, but I’m&nbsp;
sure the people who actually make pitches are well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.980
aware of them. When they see that Mark has a&nbsp;
far higher closing rate than the other Sharks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:40.680
they’ll likely be motivated to get a deal with&nbsp;
Mark. On TV, this will come of as entrepreneurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.900
consistently choosing Mark over everyone else&nbsp;
which is again great for his brand image. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.100
choosing to not complete a deal because the&nbsp;
fundamentals of the startup don’t match up is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:55.320
completely fine and perfectly understandable.&nbsp;
The problem, however, is that the downsides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:00.960
of business are rarely shown. The overwhelming&nbsp;
majority of the show has to do with showing off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.360
this idealistic vision of success. We see sharks&nbsp;
shaking hands in these big corporate rooms in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:11.400
front of these giant mansions. But, this is&nbsp;
not what happens in real life even for rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:17.220
businessmen like the sharks. Most of these guys&nbsp;
are actually pretty low key and live pretty modest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:23.520
lives, but this doesn’t align with the producers’&nbsp;
vision, so they just make stuff up. A perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:28.860
example of this is with Chris Sacca. Chris is&nbsp;
literally what this show is supposed to be about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:34.920
Venture capitalists and startups that make it&nbsp;
big. Chris was an early investor in Uber and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:41.040
made him a billionaire, so you would think that he&nbsp;
fits the mold perfectly. But, his real life wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:46.380
nearly exciting enough for the show. First the&nbsp;
producers asked to film at his house, but when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.820
they realized he lived in a 3 bedroom house in&nbsp;
Truckee, California, they were quick to back out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:55.200
Next, they asked him to film in front of his&nbsp;
private jet, but Chris doesn’t actually own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.660
a private jet. While he does fly private, he&nbsp;
finds that it’s far more economical to simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.520
rent a jet when he needs one. Hearing this, they&nbsp;
wanted to shoot in front his supercars and boats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.580
but the most expensive car that Chris owns is a&nbsp;
Tesla and he doesn’t own a boat. Running out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:17.880
options, they asked to film at his HQ, but his&nbsp;
VC firm doesn’t have a fancy HQ either. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:22.320
he does most of his deals from home to spend&nbsp;
more time with his family. According to Chris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.840
the producers quote “didn’t know how to make me&nbsp;
look rich to America.” And they told him that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.280
has to overcompensate for the shirt that he&nbsp;
wears. They eventually decided to just fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:36.480
the look. They filmed in a random conference&nbsp;
room of one of the company’s that Chris owns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.400
And they staged some sort of billionaire wine&nbsp;
celebration in someone's patio. When you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.780
struggling to portray a real VC billionaire to&nbsp;
the public, there’s no question that there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.860
something wrong with how you’re trying to portray&nbsp;
them. Instead of giving us a real look into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:54.420
lives of the sharks and the entrepreneurs, more&nbsp;
times than not, we’re just shown success p**n.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.380
THE TANK:&nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:04.680
Aside from ulterior motives and misleading visions&nbsp;
of success, what goes on in the tank is also not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.360
something to learn from. First of all, the vast&nbsp;
majority of businesses that come onto Shark Tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.060
are product oriented businesses. This isn’t by&nbsp;
random chance. The producers specifically choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.500
products that viewers can relate to. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
the businesses that generally want to be on Shark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:24.120
Tank are companies that can benefit from public&nbsp;
exposure. If your business provides back end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:28.980
support for Walmart, this wouldn’t be all that&nbsp;
fun for viewers to watch. The problem, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:34.200
is that most successful startups are exactly this.&nbsp;
When was the last time you heard about a clothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.120
or food startup that makes it to a billion&nbsp;
dollars. Almost all unicorns have to do with tech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:44.520
yet we rarely see this on the show. So, if you’re&nbsp;
a real entrepreneur, you would have a much better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.960
shot at making it if you try to fulfill some&nbsp;
niche role in tech than trying to start any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.820
the businesses on the show. If you do start&nbsp;
a business and are trying to raise money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:58.320
you shouldn’t take inspiration from Shark Tank’s&nbsp;
valuation because more times than not, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.600
rip offs. I’m sure we’re all familiar with those&nbsp;
entrepenerus that offer like 5% of their company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:10.140
for $500,000. This is a terrible idea in the tank&nbsp;
because the Sharks are gonna rip you apart. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:15.420
in the real VC world, this is extremely common.&nbsp;
Most of the businesses that we see on Shark Tank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:20.340
are in what you would call Series A Funding.&nbsp;
Series A is usually the first time entrepreneurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:25.560
ask VCs to invest. Until this point, the startup&nbsp;
is funded by the entrpreneuers and their friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.840
and family. And the average valuation of Series A&nbsp;
funding isn’t half a million or one million like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:37.440
we see on Shark Tank. The average valuation is&nbsp;
$22 million and the average raised is $13 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:42.120
Even Seed Funding rounds which come before&nbsp;
Series A have higher valuations than we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:48.600
on Shark Tank. The average seed valuation as of&nbsp;
2018 was nearly $6 million. And these businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:54.660
generally have zero revenue. So, to see businesses&nbsp;
with 6 figures if not 7 figures in sales get lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:59.700
valuations on Shark Tank is quite comical. The&nbsp;
only reason the Sharks are able to demand such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.500
high valuations is because of they’re essentially&nbsp;
celebrity endorsers. If you’re simply looking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:10.080
money, you’ll be able find far more reasonable&nbsp;
valuations amongst real VC firms. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.820
you almost certainly wont be ridiculed. VC&nbsp;
investors aren’t gonna tell you that you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.480
dead to them. If you come back to them in&nbsp;
a later round of funding with more results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:23.400
they may very well invest because all they&nbsp;
care about is returns. They don’t care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:28.560
upholding some sort of TV persona. Something&nbsp;
else to note is that you wont have 10 seconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:33.840
to accept a deal either. Honestly, this is&nbsp;
pretty ridiculous. Selling a portion of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.640
company especially at a lower valuation than&nbsp;
you asked for is a pretty big decision. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:43.200
the sharks rarely give you time to think or&nbsp;
talk about it. So, if you’re truly looking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.240
raise money, don’t take any inspiration from Shark&nbsp;
Tank because you’re just asking to be ripped off.

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:53.880
THE TRUTH:
In the end, I know this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.880 --> 00:11:00.420
video has been quite critical about Shark Tank,&nbsp;
but it’s not all bad. For many, Shark Tank may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:05.100
what motivates them to take their first step into&nbsp;
entrepreneurship. And Shark Tank does teach people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:10.080
about the basic traits that investors look for and&nbsp;
how to raise money. But, Shark Tank shouldn’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:15.060
taken any more seriously than this. Most of the&nbsp;
entrepreneurs and sharks are there for publicity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:20.820
not to make deals. Similarly, much of the show&nbsp;
is built upon this fake vision of success instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:25.980
of the real grind behind businesses. And most&nbsp;
importantly, in terms of actually raising money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:31.440
Shark Tank isn’t all that great. Now of course,&nbsp;
there are some exceptions. If you’re a product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:36.000
oriented business, want publicity, and would&nbsp;
like a celebrity businessperson on your side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.700
then Shark Tank could be an option for you. But,&nbsp;
for most entrepreneurs, tech and traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:46.320
VC firms are likely your best bets and you&nbsp;
shouldn’t take Shark Tank as anything more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.600
than just entertainment. But that’s just what&nbsp;
I think. Do you think Shark Tank should offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.880
a more realistic view into VC funding? Comment&nbsp;
that down below. Also, drop a like if you’re a fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:01.560
of Shark Tank for entertainment. And of course,&nbsp;
consider joining our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.220
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

